            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"A divinely given event, object, or act—often a miraculous wonder—that points beyond itself to authenticate a messenger, reveal God's glory, or mark a covenant or prophetic truth.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"A marked witness to God's word functioning specifically through temporal structures: either a prophetic similitude/event given in advance, or a visible covenant token, explicitly rejecting the necessity of an on-demand wonder.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"An authenticating pointer or proof, prominently featuring miracles, covenant markers, and eschatological omens.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A marked witness to God's word, strictly categorized as either a prophetic anticipation whose fulfillment testifies, or a continuing covenant token.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes immediate, on-demand wonders from the core definition, requiring signs to possess a specific temporal witness structure (either given in advance to testify upon fulfillment, or established as a continuing covenant memorial).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book rejects the common view that a sign is fundamentally a 'miraculous act' or 'wonder,' requiring even miraculous signs to function as 'lived similitudes' rather than mere displays of power.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book retains the consensus concept of a 'pointer' or 'witness' but significantly narrows the mechanics to prophetic anticipation and covenant memorials, explicitly countering the dominant consensus emphasis on signs as immediate authenticating miracles.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The Gospel of John extensively uses the term 'sign' (semeion) to describe Jesus' miracles, which function in context as immediate displays of divine power and glory designed to elicit faith, a dynamic that may be unnecessarily constrained by restricting signs strictly to prophetic anticipation or established covenant tokens.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
